BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:[Center on Democracy\, Development and the Rule of Law] Marcela Ib
 áñez — Authoritarian Aid and Democratic Attitudes: Backlash\, Not Diff
 usion
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261105T120000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261105T131500
DTSTAMP:20261105T202933Z
UID:stanford_campus-localist-54004024867636-54004031461183@local-events
DESCRIPTION:How does the local presence of an authoritarian power shape ci
 tizens' commitment to democracy? Existing theories suggest engagement with
  authoritarian states should weaken democratic commitment through norm dif
 fusion\, accommodation\, or authoritarian learning. This paper challenges 
 that expectation by examining Chinese development finance in Latin America
 . Using geocoded data\, it estimates the subnational effect of exposure to
  Chinese aid on democratic attitudes. Exposure increases support for de\n
 \nAges: All ages\nSource: https://cddrl.fsi.stanford.edu/events/marcela-ib
 anez-cddrl-research-seminar\nLast synced: 2026-11-05 12:29 PST
URL:https://cddrl.fsi.stanford.edu/events/marcela-ibanez-cddrl-research-se
 minar
END:VEVENT
